Bug: A malformed __has_include in an #if stops the server

`#if __has_include(1)`, or `__has_include "a.h"`, or `__has_include(<a.h` without
the `>`, fails the internal check `fileTable.tab[fileNumber] != NULL` (filetable.c:68)
right after handleFoundSymbolReference(), and the server exits. The same for
__has_include_next. Text being typed passes through these states.

Every path that fails is one where handleHasIncludeOp() or handleHasIncludeNextOp()
(src/yylex.c) returns 0 before the closing ')'. The paths that read on to the ')'
are fine (test_parsing_has_include_errors). So the 0, an end of input to the
cppexp parser, is probably what leaves the rest of the line to be lexed as C, but
that is not confirmed.

When fixed, the six warningMessage() calls on these paths, still guarded by
`options.mode!=ServerMode`, can become parsingMessageAt(position, ...) like the
')' ones, and be asserted here.
